/**
 * Returns the sum of Opportunity.Amount for the four open-pipeline stages
 * within the inclusive CloseDate window [fromDate, toDate].
 *
 * One aggregate query instead of four per-stage queries:
 *   • Same logical result, but one SOQL round-trip rather than four.
 *   • Stage filtering is done with a bind-variable IN list, so the
 *     "no SOQL in a loop" guideline is respected without an exception.
 *   • Null Amounts are naturally skipped by SUM(); a stage that has no
 *     rows (or only null-Amount rows) simply won't appear in the
 *     AggregateResult set, so we pre-seed every key to 0.
 */
public with sharing class PipelineSummary {

    public static Map<String, Decimal> totalsByStage(Date fromDate, Date toDate) {

        // Seed all four keys to 0 so the contract "never null" holds
        // even when no Opportunities match.
        List<String> stages = new List<String>{
            'Prospecting',
            'Qualification',
            'Proposal/Price Quote',
            'Negotiation/Review'
        };

        Map<String, Decimal> result = new Map<String, Decimal>();
        for (String s : stages) {
            result.put(s, 0);
        }

        // Single aggregate query: GROUP BY gives us one row per stage
        // that actually has matching records.
        for (AggregateResult ar : [
            SELECT  StageName, SUM(Amount) total
            FROM    Opportunity
            WHERE   StageName IN :stages
              AND   CloseDate  >= :fromDate
              AND   CloseDate  <= :toDate
            GROUP BY StageName
        ]) {
            // SUM(Amount) is null when every Amount in the group is null
            // (or the group doesn't appear at all – already handled by the
            //  0-seed above).  Guard against the null-to-zero case.
            Decimal total = (Decimal) ar.get('total');
            result.put(
                (String) ar.get('StageName'),
                total != null ? total : 0
            );
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

### Why one query instead of four?

| | 4 × `SUM(Amount)` in a loop | 1 × `GROUP BY` |
|---|---|---|
| SOQL calls | 4 | 1 |
| Governor-limit headroom | uses 4 of 100 | uses 1 of 100 |
| "No SOQL in a loop" | needs the explicit exception you noted | satisfied outright |
| Readability | clear | equally clear; the `IN` list makes intent explicit |
| Correctness | identical | identical |

Four queries would be perfectly acceptable in production (fixed, tiny N). I just default to the single-query shape because it costs nothing extra and keeps the code within the letter of the guideline rather than an exception to it. If you or your team prefer the four-query loop for readability reasons, that's a reasonable style choice too — nothing breaks either way.

