/**
 * Asynchronous "give us feedback" task creation for newly closed cases.
 *
 * Accepts a list of Case Ids (one or many).  Each case is processed
 * independently so a failure on one record does not prevent the others
 * from receiving their survey task.  A future vendor integration callout
 * can be added inside the per-case try/catch without changing the
 * trigger or queuing strategy.
 */
public class CaseSurveyJob implements Queueable {

    @TestVisible
    private final List<Id> caseIds;

    public CaseSurveyJob(List<Id> caseIds) {
        this.caseIds = caseIds;
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {

        // Re-query to pick up any changes that happened between the
        // trigger firing and the async execution (e.g. a parallel
        // automation re-opened the case, or the contact was changed).
        List<Case> cases = [
            SELECT Id, CaseNumber, ContactId, IsClosed
            FROM Case
            WHERE Id IN :caseIds
              AND IsClosed = TRUE
              AND ContactId != NULL
            FOR UPDATE
        ];

        if (cases.isEmpty()) return;

        // Build tasks, wrapping per-case construction in try/catch so a
        // data problem on one case (null CaseNumber after a migration, etc.)
        // doesn't block the rest.  The future vendor integration will
        // naturally live inside this loop.
        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        Date surveyDate = Date.today().addDays(2);

        for (Case c : cases) {
            try {
                tasks.add(new Task(
                    WhatId       = c.Id,
                    WhoId        = c.ContactId,
                    Subject      = 'CSAT survey: ' + c.CaseNumber,
                    ActivityDate = surveyDate,
                    Status       = 'Not Started',
                    Type         = 'Email'
                ));
            } catch (Exception ex) {
                // Log and continue – isolation between cases.
                System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                    'CaseSurveyJob: skipped case ' + c.Id + ' – ' + ex.getMessage());
            }
        }

        if (tasks.isEmpty()) return;

        // Bulk DML, allOrNone = false → one bad row won't roll back the batch.
        try {
            Database.SaveResult[] results = Database.insert(tasks, false);

            // Surface any row-level failures in the async transaction log
            // so operations can trace them.
            for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
                if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                    System.debug(LoggingLevel.WARN,
                        'CaseSurveyJob: Task insert failed for task index ' + i
                        + ' – ' + results[i].getErrors()[0].getMessage());
                }
            }
        } catch (Exception ex) {
            // DML-wide failure (e.g. org-wide permission issue).  Log for
            // investigation; the next nightly run or manual retry can recover.
            System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                'CaseSurveyJob: bulk Task insert threw – ' + ex.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

### Quick reference: what changed vs. a naïve "one `enqueueJob` per case" loop

| Concern | Naïve approach | This implementation |
|---|---|---|
| 200-case bulk close | Throws at job #51; entire trigger DML rolls back | Two Queueable jobs (50 + 50 …); trigger DML succeeds |
| Isolation | ✔ (by design) | ✔ – each Queueable processes its chunk independently; within a chunk, per-case `try/catch` + `allOrNone=false` keep failures contained |
| Stale state | Assumes trigger-time data is still valid | Re-queries `IsClosed = TRUE AND ContactId != NULL` before building tasks |
| Vendor integration (future) | Would need a redesign to add per-case callouts inside a single shared transaction | Already scaffolded: drop the callout inside the existing per-case `try/catch` loop |
| `FOR UPDATE` | N/A | Pessimistic lock inside the Queueable prevents a concurrent re-open from racing the Task creation |

## Critique of the approach

The per-loop `Messaging.sendEmail` call is **counterproductive in production** for two reasons:

1. **Governor limit.** The per-transaction cap is **10 `Messaging.sendEmail` invocations** *and* **10 single emails**. A 200-opportunity batch will hit the limit on iteration 11, throwing a `System.LimitException` that aborts the *entire* transaction—meaning zero emails are sent, which is strictly worse than a "single call with a list" (where individual failures are reported in `SendEmailResult` without aborting). The fault-isolation you wanted is illusory once the limit is breached.

2. **No real fault isolation benefit.** `Messaging.sendEmail` on a one-element list still throws the same exceptions (e.g. "no email address on record") that would abort the batch. A list call gives you per-recipient `isSuccess()`/`getMessages()` so you can log or retry failures *without* hitting the 10-call limit.

The bulk-safe, limit-safe pattern is one `Messaging.sendEmail` call with all messages in a list, then iterate the results. I've implemented your requested signature and shape below, but structured the inner call so you can see both. I've added a TODO comment at the call site explaining the production concern.

